# Locks, alarms, covers and valves

Name the lock, alarm, cover or valve, show the state it reports and say what the button will do. These controls change who can get in or move equipment. A card colour, a confirmation dialog or a hidden button never proves that someone has permission or that the home is safe.

This page is contract HA-CTRL-006. Its examples are simulated. They do not connect to Home Assistant or operate equipment.

<h2 id="ha-ctrl-006-capabilities">Task and capability contract</h2>

Offer only the actions the entity supports. Keep the native confirmation and code flow.

| Equipment | What to do |
| --- | --- |
| Lock | Keep Lock, Unlock and Open (latch release) as separate actions, and offer Open only if the lock supports it. Keep protected calls, code requirements and native confirmation. |
| Alarm panel | Offer only the arming modes the panel supports, through the native code flow. Arming, pending and triggered are states of the panel, not an on/off switch. |
| Cover | Keep open, close, position, tilt and Stop as the cover supports them. A partly open cover is not fully open. |
| Valve | Keep the device class, position and Stop as the valve supports them. A position is not a flow measurement or proof that a pipe is isolated. |
| Permission or code missing | Explain the restriction through the native flow. Do not store a substitute code in card configuration or documentation. |

<h2 id="ha-ctrl-006-states">States, pending work and failure</h2>

Show the state the integration reports: locked, unlocked, locking, unlocking, open, opening, jammed, unknown or unavailable. Do not treat an unlocked lock as an open door. A separate contact sensor can report the door, but that is a different reading.

While a cover or valve moves, keep Stop within reach if it is supported. Closing a review or leaving the page does not stop the movement.

| Outcome | Say |
| --- | --- |
| Home Assistant rejected the request | The request was rejected. Keep the last reported state visible. |
| The waiting limit passed with no new state | Not confirmed. The action may still complete. Do not report success or undo it. |
| The lock reports jammed | Jammed, with the reported time. Do not retry the lock, close a valve or disarm an alarm automatically. |

Follow the panel's own timing for alarm delays. Do not invent a countdown. Do not turn a camera image, a presence report or a closed-valve icon into “Home secure”.

<h2 id="ha-ctrl-006-content">Content and interpretation</h2>

Name the object in the action, as in “Unlock front door” or “Stop study blind”. Describe latch release separately from unlocking. Write “Reported closed” when the only evidence is a device report.

Keep code prompts private. Do not echo a code into a notification, log, example or screenshot. Keep native translations and the source's fault wording.

<h2 id="ha-ctrl-006-accessibility">Accessibility and interaction</h2>

Give every action a keyboard route and keep Stop visible during movement. A review dialog needs an accessible name, a logical focus order, Escape or dismiss behaviour, and a return of focus to the control that opened it. The local dialog shows only how a review looks.

Do not stack a whole-card tap action over separate lock buttons. Show faults as lasting text. Reserve assertive announcements for a defined urgent condition.

<h2 id="ha-ctrl-006-integration">Native integration boundary</h2>

Keep the native More info dialog and its protected lock and alarm flows. This page adds no access-control layer. Home Assistant must still reject a request the person is not allowed to make, even if the interface is bypassed. Home Assistant does not replace physical safety checks, a certified alarm or the equipment's own procedures.

